#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046644} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518259} :activity/id #uuid "4d9716c2-538f-569a-b8a0-0013d0209d04" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048966 :topic/id #uuid "cfd9de67-b6d3-5aac-81ab-498c732e22a6" :topic/math-academy-id 6720} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518240 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518147 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1dd368cc-9a2b-551e-b564-ee313f513e46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517541} {:answer/id #uuid "20deeaf4-0740-55aa-9dcb-2e6f140632ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517545} {:answer/id #uuid "79c50765-9d84-524e-b588-8c069de3e84d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517717} {:answer/id #uuid "d6d57042-0b74-581c-8b12-7c36887caccc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517895} {:answer/id #uuid "fb517908-0db1-516e-8b98-2030ddb3dcdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517961}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517895} :answer-field/id #uuid "eff11dfc-8ed3-50df-b798-7c071f983a45" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518087}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5fd4a0bf-2844-5191-8ab1-3fd5afa7e748" :question/math-academy-id "q-343666" :question/problem "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with periodically forced harmonic motion without damping is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+18y=6{sin}^{2}⁡pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Which value of the parameter $p$ causes the oscillator to resonate?" :question/worked-solution "The equation of forced harmonic motion without damping is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=18\\,⟹\\,\\omega =3\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\sqrt{2}t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\sqrt{2}t).\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency of the external periodic force $f(t)=6{sin}^{2}⁡pt$ is equal to the frequency of the complementary solution, which is $\\omega =3\\sqrt{2}.$ Since \n\n$$\nf(t)=6{sin}^{2}⁡pt=3-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡2pt,\n$$\n\nthe frequency of the external periodic force is $2p.$\n\nTherefore, the oscillator experiences resonance when $p=\\frac{\\omega }{2}=\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$"} :step/id #uuid "373883cf-0b34-53c8-a1b3-d0897fad83b2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518266}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518259 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518148 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a645be0-c56e-5fba-b4a4-a98456908295" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517602} {:answer/id #uuid "47adda2a-334d-5928-b439-90caf6b3f1a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517627} {:answer/id #uuid "5cf5111e-dd21-52f0-ba55-7a49bf7ac9de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517657} {:answer/id #uuid "8227aecf-efb6-5968-88a7-e86a8151635a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517736} {:answer/id #uuid "b985565e-b931-58c3-9ed9-2657a34e2b19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517836}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517657} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb900ce9-eab3-5acd-b944-c83320b552a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518096}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6127495a-5135-5ebc-930f-1823afa46170" :question/math-academy-id "q-94240" :question/problem "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with periodically forced harmonic motion without damping is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+3y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡pt+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Which value of the parameter $p$ causes the oscillator to resonate?" :question/worked-solution "The equation of forced harmonic motion without damping is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=3\\,⟹\\,\\omega =\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{3}t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\sqrt{3}t.\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency $p$ of the external periodic force $f(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡pt+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡pt$ coincides with the frequency of the complementary solution, which is $\\omega =\\sqrt{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the oscillator experiences resonance when $p=\\sqrt{3}.$"} :step/id #uuid "6ea7ee19-1db2-5615-9f9e-e31df6e39383" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518240}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518266 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495094 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f1673e8-dffa-58ad-a21d-370ac4f21d2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494409}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494409} :answer-field/id #uuid "e18cc19c-c2dd-5d83-85f6-24d5473b0766" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495028}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "64d62ce0-8437-5a77-8100-0fa04bf9d1d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-343690" :question/problem "A particle of mass $0.8\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. An external periodic force \n\n$$\nF(t)=(4{cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{5}{2}t)-2)\\,\\text{N}\n$$\n\nis applied to the particle.\n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion without damping and the system experiences resonance, what is the spring constant $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}${\\text{kg/s}}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with forced harmonic motion without damping under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ and external force $F(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky+F(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m$ and $F(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n0.8{y}^{″}=-ky+(4{cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{5}{2}t)-2).\n$$\n\nSimplifying and rearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{5k}{4}y=\\frac{5}{2}(2{cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{5}{2}t)-1).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration, and $\\omega $ is the system's internal frequency.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{5k}{4}\\,⟹\\,k=\\frac{4}{5}{\\omega }^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency of the external periodic force $f(t)=\\frac{5}{2}(2{cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{5}{2}t)-1)$ is equal to the frequency of the complementary solution $\\omega .$ Since\n\n$$\nf(t)=\\frac{5}{2}(2{cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{5}{2}t)-1)=\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t),\n$$\n\nthe frequency of the external periodic force is $5.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{4}{5}{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{4}{5}\\cdot {5}^{2}=20\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "88f88f3d-b294-5d78-b139-7b4adde6210f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518317}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518302 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317433 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f8deee66-8faa-5103-9ffb-a0528de31c35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467280} {:answer/id #uuid "8efe3b1c-13b5-58c1-8319-811c069ec943" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467281}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186467281} :answer-field/id #uuid "791e5bec-2bbe-5ef0-be17-7936c37a397d" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468361} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b11162f-ed10-562f-a582-635aca5f072e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467282} {:answer/id #uuid "c1df0d29-07b6-5d2b-a737-504fb58038dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467283}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186467283} :answer-field/id #uuid "930db752-9805-5bd5-8731-4cec255544bb" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468362} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "db48d1bd-0f55-5b7e-8868-e1d284da68cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467284} {:answer/id #uuid "1a443ce3-df82-501c-a748-ef96d7ff6e86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467285}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186467285} :answer-field/id #uuid "80f14578-0fa8-5566-9fd0-14c07a82fade" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468363}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7120fcf0-d3de-513a-93a7-21786701d7c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-343764" :question/problem "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion without damping. The particle is acted on by an external periodic force\n$f(t)=10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t).$\nAssuming the system experiences resonance, which of the following could be used to represent the general solution for the position $y(t)?$\n\nI. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)+\\frac{5}{3}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\nII. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+ϕ)+\\frac{5}{3}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\nIII. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+\\frac{5}{3}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R,$ and $ϕ$ are constants.\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Because the motion is undamped and the system experiences resonance, the forcing frequency equals the natural frequency $\\omega .$\n\nThe external force is $f(t)=10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t),$ which is sinusoidal with frequency $3.$ Therefore,\n$\\omega =3.$\n\nThe complementary solution for the associated simple harmonic motion equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n1\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)$\n\n2\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+ϕ)$\n\n3\\. ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\nAt resonance, a sinusoidal trial at frequency $\\omega $ duplicates the complementary solution, so we multiply by $t$ and take a resonant particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)).\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution and $\\omega =3$ into the differential equation, ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[(6D-9Ct)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-(6C+9Dt)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)]+9t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) & =10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\ 6D\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-6C\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & =10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients, $C=0$ and $D=\\frac{5}{3}.$ \n\nSo, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\frac{5}{3}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n1\\. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)+\\frac{5}{3}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\n2\\. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+ϕ)+\\frac{5}{3}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\n3\\. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+\\frac{5}{3}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\nSolutions I, II, and III match the forms above.\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"."} :step/id #uuid "d88d67e5-0c3f-5914-b3bd-6949d7868fd1"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518317 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317423 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aaaa23f1-3d6f-5700-8adf-0ab757d01cc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316079}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316079} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b00e565-5dc1-5b1f-8a94-9d5cc2730e81" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316864}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c9d9c170-a8c4-5910-b604-3da53c4eb5a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-343699" :question/problem "A particle of mass $0.5\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. An external periodic force \n\n$$\nF(t)=2{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{N}\n$$\n\nis applied to the particle.\n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion without damping and the system experiences resonance, what is the spring constant $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}${\\text{kg/s}}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with forced harmonic motion without damping under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ and external force $F(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky+F(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m$ and $F(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n0.5{y}^{″}=-ky+2{cos}^{2}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nSimplifying and rearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2ky=4{cos}^{2}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration, and $\\omega $ is the system's internal frequency.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=2k\\,⟹\\,k=\\frac{1}{2}{\\omega }^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency of the external periodic force $f(t)=4{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)$ is equal to the frequency of the complementary solution $\\omega .$ Since\n\n$$\nf(t)=4{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)=2(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)),\n$$\n\nthe frequency of the external periodic force is $4.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{1}{2}{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {4}^{2}=8\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "f52a8e3f-5139-540f-bde0-174c3dc62d36" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518302}}] :activity/title "Resonance in Vibrating Systems" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517486}]}
